## 💻 Code Se Samjho: Dono Mein Antar

Chalo ek chota sa test code dekhte hain jo is cheez ko bilkul saaf kar dega.

### 1. Synchronous Execution (Line-by-Line)

Isme jab tak beech wali line poori khatam nahi hoti, niche wala code wait karta hai.

```javascript
console.log("1. Order liya");

// Maan lo ye ek heavy loop hai jo 3 second leta hai (Blocking Code)
for (let i = 0; i < 3000000000; i++) {} 

console.log("2. Khana ban gaya");
console.log("3. Bill de diya");

```

**Output Sequence:** `1` $\rightarrow$ *(3 second ka freeze)* $\rightarrow$ `2` $\rightarrow$ `3`.

### 2. Asynchronous Execution (Background Task)

Ab hum Node.js ke built-in `setTimeout` ka use karke is task ko background mein bhejte hain.

```javascript
console.log("1. Order liya");

// Yeh background mein chala gaya, server ruka nahi
setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("2. Khana ban gaya (Background task complete)");
}, 3000); // 3 second ka delay

console.log("3. Agle customer ka order liya");

```

### 🧠 Ab Output Sequence Socho!

JavaScript is code ko aise chalayega:

1. `1. Order liya` print hoga.
2. `setTimeout` dekhte hi JS use background timer ko saump dega aur aage badh jayega.
3. `3. Agle customer ka order liya` turant print ho jayega!
4. Jaise hi 3 second poore honge, background se `2. Khana ban gaya` ka output screen par aayega.

**Actual Output:**

```text
1. Order liya
3. Agle customer ka order liya
2. Khana ban gaya (Background task complete)

```

